feat(ci): gate the exemptions IP §3 says not to build

doc 01 §3 says "Do not build these. If you find yourself building them, stop."
A rule about what a repository must NOT grow has no failing test to write:
nothing breaks the day someone adds a policy engine. It stops being true
quietly, in a pull request that looks like a feature, and the only thing that
can notice is something reading the repository on every change.

Running what already existed found a gap rather than a failure. Of the eight
exemptions, two are genuinely asserted — E4 by LED-011, MCP-044..046 and the
TestE4 cases, E8 by TestE8TheWrapperHoldsNoKeyMaterial — and nine such cases
pass. The other six were prose.

Four of those six are checkable as absence and are now checked: no policy
engine (E1), no SPIFFE federation surface (E5), no blockchain or
ledger-database product (E6), no backfill of pre-adoption history (E7).

TWO ARE NOT CHECKABLE AND THE GATE SAYS SO rather than claiming them. E2 is
"attestation proves what a workload is, not that it behaves" — an absence of a
claim, not of code, with no string whose presence would mean the claim had been
made. E3 is "do not chase the badge", which would look like ordinary work on
verification. A gate pretending to cover those is worse than one naming them as
review's job.

Comments are not code, and that half matters as much as the rules. This
repository discusses its own exemptions constantly and must be able to: doc 01
itself says the design "must not preclude federation". A gate that fired on the
word would make the exemption unmentionable, and the first fix anyone reached
for would be deleting the explanation. So every rule strips comment lines and
test files are out of scope.

The gate excludes itself and its self-test, by two literal paths, because both
must contain the strings they hunt for — it reported four breaches on its own
files on the first run. no-operator-paths.sh carries the measurement of an
exclusion that was too wide and produced a false pass in the gate whose whole
job was to prevent one, so this one is paths rather than a directory, and the
self-test plants a breach in a different script and requires it caught.

That self-test is the reason to believe any of this. The gate passes on this
repository today and is meant to, which is exactly the condition in which a gate
can be worthless: every rule could be misspelled and the output identical.

